    "body": "2026 SEMA Show • 2027 Toyota Crown Signia Gets GT-S Concept Treatment for SEMA, Becomes a Sleek Wagon\nElectra Closer to Ultra Short Aircraft Operations in New York City\n8 photos\nPhoto: Electra\nAdvanced Air Mobility (AAM) isn’t limited to eVTOLs (electric vertical takeoff and landing). Electra’s EL9 Ultra Short combines hybrid-electric propulsion with an innovative take on fixed-wing design. The EL9 is like a greener regional aircraft boasting amazing versatility in terms of infrastructure, which translates to greater access and better efficiency, and all of these qualities could be soon experienced first-hand by folks in New York City.\nAir travel from New York City to multiple communities across the region could become much faster, greener, and overall more efficient. A new partnership between Electra, the company behind the EL9 Ultra Short aircraft, and VertiPorts by Atlantic is already looking into the best infrastructure opportunities to support regular Ultra Short operations in the area.\nAs its name implies, the EL9 Ultra Short’s greatest advantage is the ability to take off and land within just 150 feet (45.7 meters). This means helicopter-like access for a fixed-wing aircraft that doesn’t have vertical takeoff and landing capabilities. Also, with a nine-passenger configuration and hybrid-electric propulsion that supports an extended range, EL9 checks the boxes of an efficient regional aircraft.\nThis innovative design is at the heart of Electra’s “Direct Aviation” concept, defined as a “new model of point-to-point regional travel.” It doesn’t require dedicated infrastructure built from scratch, just distributed access points added to an existing network, in key locations.\nElectra ’s collaboration with this new infrastructure partner will focus specifically on new and existing VertiPorts by Atlantic sites in New York City, and how various types of infrastructure, such as barges and piers, could be integrated at these sites for Ultra Short operations.\nVertiPorts by Atlantic is the vertiport subsidiary of Atlantic Aviation, an established operator with a large network of fixed base operations (FBOs) at more than 100 locations across North America. Through this subsidiary, Atlantic Aviation is expanding its expertise and actual network to develop and operate vertiport infrastructure in support of AAM.\nDirect Aviation is ultimately about making regular connections between city hubs faster and more efficient. Electra’s recently-issued Direct Aviation Market Outlook estimates, among other things, that approximately one million passengers per day could benefit from this type of air mobility service within the first decade of operations. However, that requires a sufficient number of aircraft (from 12,000 to 16,000) and a solid network of distributed access points.\nElectra has what it takes to make this happen, in cooperation with industry partners and with the support of an adequate regulatory framework. Earlier this year, it was named the Premier Private Participant in the eIPP (eVTOL Integration Pilot Program), America’s historic Advanced Air Mobility initiative.\nElectra’s recent demonstration at Downtown Skyport in Lower Manhattan was most likely the first time a fixed-wing aircraft without vertical capabilities landed at the heliport.\nElectra’s flagship, the EL9 Ultra Short, is currently aiming for full Type Certification with FAA (the Federal Aviation Administration). First flights are set to begin in late 2027 or early 2028.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nultra short aircraft hybrid-electric aircraft electra aero EL9 Ultra Short\nRead Press Release\nAbout the author: Otilia Drăgan\nOtilia believes that if it’s eco, green, or groundbreaking, people should know about it (especially if it's got wheels or wings). Working in online media for over five years, she's gained a deeper perspective on how people everywhere can inspire each other. Full profile\nUP NEXT Most Powerful Harley-Davidson Road Glide Gets Even Meaner for the 2027 Bagger World Cup",
